 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$ depending on the unique-finding mechanism. Sorting-based approaches (like `np.unique`) are $O(N \log N)$ [S2, unofficial]. GPU implementations using sort-scatter can achieve $O(N \log N)$ with high parallelism [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(M \cdot F)$ for the output grid, where $M$ is the number of occupied voxels and $F$ is the feature dimension. The input is $O(N \cdot F)$. Sparse implementations (Voxel Hashing) reduce memory usage for empty space [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** A parallel CUDA implementation runs in ~0.04s per frame on CPU-equivalent logic but offloads to GPU for speed [S3, unofficial]. The GPU sort-scatter variant provides a ~100× speedup over sequential CPU implementations [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Deterministic output order; handles unstructured data; reduces density significantly; suitable for neural network inputs.
-*   **Limitations:** Fixed grid size may lead to wasted memory if sparse; boundary handling requires care; non-deterministic variants are faster but not reproducible [S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When reproducible downsampling is required for debugging or training stability; when converting point clouds to regular grids for CNNs.
-*   **When not to use:** When memory efficiency for extremely sparse data is critical (use Voxel Hashing); when max-pooling features is preferred (use Grid Pooling).
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ for coordinate mapping and aggregation, where $N$ is the number of points. The deterministic unique-sort step adds a factor related to the number of unique voxels $M$, typically $O(M \log M)$ or $O(M)$ with hash-based unique [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(D_x D_y D_z)$ for the dense grid if pre-allocated, or $O(M)$ for the output structures where $M$ is the number of occupied voxels [S1, S3, unofficial]. The sparse variant (Voxel Hashing) uses $O(M)$ memory [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Sequential CPU implementations (e.g., using `std::unordered_map`) are slow for large $N$ [S1, unofficial]. GPU implementations (CUDA) provide significant speedups, with deterministic sort-scatter methods achieving ~100× speedup over naive CPU while maintaining reproducibility [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Deterministic output order; simple implementation; suitable for dense grids; handles arbitrary attributes [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Memory usage grows with grid resolution; empty space is wasted in dense grids; boundary handling requires care [S1, S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When reproducible downsampling is required for debugging or training stability; when the point cloud density is high and a fixed grid size is acceptable [S1, S3, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When memory is constrained and the cloud is very sparse (use Voxel Hashing); when speed is critical and determinism is not required (use Non-Deterministic GPU variant) [S1, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Hard Voxelization is a fundamental technique in 3D computer vision and point cloud processing. It is widely used in autonomous driving datasets (e.g., nuScenes, KITTI) for preprocessing LiDAR data [S1, S3, unofficial]. The method is implemented in various libraries, including PointCloud Workflows (PCW) and PointCloud Core [S2, unofficial], and is a core component of many 3D object detection networks (e.g., SECOND, PV-RCNN) which use voxelization to convert sparse LiDAR points into dense voxel grids for 3D CNNs [S1, S3, unofficial]. The deterministic variant was developed to address the non-reproducibility issues of atomic-based GPU implementations, ensuring consistent results across different hardware and software environments [S3, unofficial].
+Hard Voxelization is a fundamental technique in point cloud processing, originating from the need to convert unstructured 3D data into a regular grid format for convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and volume rendering [S1, unofficial][S4]. The method is adapted from functions like `voxels()` originally written by J. Antonio Guzmán Q. for the `rTLS` package [S4]. It is widely used in autonomous driving datasets (e.g., nuScenes, KITTI) for 3D object detection [S1, S3, unofficial]. The deterministic variant was developed to address the non-reproducibility of atomic-based GPU implementations, ensuring consistent results across different hardware and software versions [S3, unofficial].
 
